module sh.sysl.llhttp.c
@tests

// The survey's questions, asked of the real compiler.
//
// Every number below was measured with a C program compiled against the vendored header before it
// was written here, so a disagreement means sysl and clang see llhttp differently -- which is the
// only failure this file is looking for.

// -- The parser's storage ----------------------------------------------------------------------

// **`llhttp_t` is 96 bytes and wants 8**, which is what lets the layer above carry it as twelve
// `u64`s: an array of `u64` is 8-aligned by construction, where a `[96]u8` promises nothing.
//
// A future llhttp with another field changes the first number and this test with it. That is the
// point of asserting it -- the alignment is the one that would corrupt silently.
@test("the parser fits in the words the layer above reserves")
parser_storage_is_twelve_aligned_words()
    assert_eq(PARSER_SIZE, 96)
    assert_eq(PARSER_ALIGN, 8)
    assert_eq(PARSER_WORDS, 12)
end parser_storage_is_twelve_aligned_words

// **This is the load-bearing one.** `Settings` is declared field by field in sysl rather than left
// opaque, because every one of its members has to be written and llhttp offers no setter. That is
// safe only while sysl's layout and C's agree, and a struct of nothing but pointers is the easiest
// case there is -- so what could actually go wrong is a *count*: an llhttp that grew a twenty-sixth
// callback would have sysl writing into 200 bytes of a 208-byte struct and leaving the last one
// holding whatever was there.
//
// Comparing the two sizes catches exactly that, and catches it at the first `sysl test` after the
// vendored copy is updated rather than at a call into an uninitialised pointer.
@test("the sysl Settings struct is the size C says llhttp_settings_t is")
settings_layout_agrees_with_c()
    assert_eq(sizeof(Settings), SETTINGS_SIZE)
    assert_eq(SETTINGS_SIZE, 200)
end settings_layout_agrees_with_c

// The two fields reached by offset rather than by an accessor. Asked of the C compiler, so they
// follow a reordering on their own; asserted here so that a reordering is *visible* rather than
// merely survived.
@test("the fields with no accessor are where C says they are")
field_offsets_are_the_headers()
    assert_eq(CONTENT_LENGTH_OFFSET, 64)
    assert_eq(FLAGS_OFFSET, 82)
end field_offsets_are_the_headers

// -- The constants -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A `c const` reads an `enum` constant and not only a `#define`, which is what makes this binding
// shim-free: llhttp's errors, methods and types are all enumerations.
@test("the enumerations read as their C values")
enum_constants_are_read()
    assert_eq(TYPE_BOTH, 0)
    assert_eq(TYPE_REQUEST, 1)
    assert_eq(TYPE_RESPONSE, 2)

    assert_eq(OK, 0)
    assert_eq(PAUSED, 21)
    assert_eq(PAUSED_UPGRADE, 22)
end enum_constants_are_read
